//! `tree/sites.av` — a SITE is one text whose words read state. The compiler
//! writes it beside the node it belongs to, for a text it can place exactly:
//! the `id` is the literal's own site (the number `@std/meta`'s `Site` holds),
//! `words` recomputes the whole text from the state as it stands, and `cells`
//! are the state cells that text reads, fixed for the site's life. A text the
//! compiler cannot place carries none, and the full paint speaks for it.
//! The page that watches a site holds what it observes (the runtime takes its
//! own reference to an observed cell), so a cell cannot die under a site.

/// One text whose words read state.
export type Site = { id: int, words: fn() -> string, cells: List<ptr> }
